# Where the browser calls the API.
#
# 127.0.0.1, matching the dev backend (`python app.py` on :5000) and the
# loopback-only Vite server in vite.config.js. Everything runs on this one
# machine, so the address the page is served from and the address it calls are
# the same host.
#
# Literally 127.0.0.1, not the name "localhost". WSL and Docker Desktop both
# listen on :5000 over IPv6 (`wslrelay` on ::1, `com.docker.backend` on ::),
# and Windows resolves localhost to ::1 first — so a request to
# http://localhost:5000 reaches one of those and is closed, never touching
# Flask, which app.py binds on IPv4 0.0.0.0. The numeric address skips name
# resolution and lands on the right process.
#
# This was previously a LAN address so a staff member on another machine could
# open an emailed link and have the app call back to a reachable API. That
# arrangement is off: nothing outside this laptop can reach the dev servers
# now. To turn it back on, put this laptop's `ipconfig` address here and in
# backend/config/dev.ini (frontend_base_url and cors_origins), set `host: true`
# in vite.config.js, and restart both dev servers — Vite reads this file only
# at startup.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5000/api
